Schoenstatt Link Privacy Policy
===============================

Last updated: 2026-09-25. First published: 2016-03-15.

Who is responsible
------------------

Schoenstatt Link is run by Jeff Roedel, who is responsible for the
personal data described here. Write to
[webmaster@schoenstatt.link](mailto:webmaster@schoenstatt.link) for
anything on this page: a question, a copy of your data, a correction, or
an erasure.

What we keep, and why
---------------------

-   **The register of the Schoenstatt Movement.** For each person: name,
    title, community and tags (such as *priest*), country, preferred
    language, date of death if they have died, public notes, and their
    **assignments**, which are the roles they hold or have held in the
    movement's associations, with start and end dates. This is what the
    site is for: knowing who serves where, so that members can find each
    other. We keep it in the movement's legitimate interest in organising
    itself (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)). We keep **no** dates of birth, name days or
    dates of ordination.

-   **Contact data** for those persons: email addresses, telephone
    numbers, web and social-media contacts, postal addresses and contact
    notes. The purpose and basis are the same as above. Only moderators
    can see it (see below), and it is deleted five years after the
    person's last activity (see *How long*).

-   **Accounts.** If you sign in: your email address, a username and
    display name, your preferred language, the roles an administrator has
    given you, and any comments you write. Signing in uses a link sent to
    your email address; there is no password. We keep your account because
    you asked for it (Art. 6(1)(b)), until you ask us to delete it.

-   **Library loans**, for the libraries that lend through this site:
    which book, when it was borrowed, renewed and returned, and by whom. A
    borrower does not need an account; the overdue notice carries a link
    to their own loans that works for 30 days. We keep this to run the
    library (Art. 6(1)(b) and (f)).

-   **The history of every record**: each change, and which account made
    it, so that mistakes can be traced and undone (Art. 6(1)(f)).

-   **No IP addresses and no User-Agent strings.** Page views are counted
    per record, without recording who viewed them. The one exception is
    deliberate: when a page fails, the error report keeps a *shortened*
    address, with the final part removed, so that it identifies a network
    rather than a device. It tells us whether a fault affects one visitor
    or everyone, is never shown on any page, and is deleted once the fault
    has not recurred for 90 days.
    Separately, the web host keeps a standard access log, outside this
    application, in which each request's full address appears for a
    limited period.

Cookies
-------

The site sets **no cookie** until you accept the cookie notice. After
that it sets one cookie that remembers your consent, and one session
cookie, which keeps you signed in. There is no analytics, advertising or
social-media tracking of any kind.

Who can see what
----------------

-   **Anyone**, without signing in, can see the shrine pages, the
    literature catalogue and the public library catalogues. Where a
    shrine lists its contact persons, visitors who are not signed in see a
    first name and the initials of the last name.

-   **Anyone signed in** can also see every association's page, with its
    roles and the full names of the people who hold them, and can search
    those contacts by name. Email addresses and telephone numbers are not
    shown to them. Anyone can create an account, so treat this level as
    public to the movement. Anyone signed in can also see the names in the
    checkout form of a library that lets anyone borrow.

-   **Moderators**, appointed by the administrators, can open person
    records, including contact data, and add and change people and
    assignments.

-   **Library administrators** can see the loans and borrowers of the
    libraries.

-   **Administrators** can see everything.

How long
--------

Contact data (email addresses, telephone numbers, web and social-media
contacts and postal addresses) is deleted five years after the person's
last activity: the end of their last assignment, their last library
loan, or the last change to their contact data, whichever is latest.
Nothing is deleted while an assignment is still active or a borrowed book
is still out. The same applies to the copies of that data in the record
of past edits.

An account is kept until you ask us to delete it. Error reports are
deleted once the fault has not recurred for 90 days. Backups taken before
a database change are deleted after at most 30 days.

Your rights
-----------

**A copy of your data.** Anyone signed in can download everything
Schoenstatt Link holds about them, as a
[JSON](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON) file, from *My data* in the
account menu. It includes the person record linked to the account, its
assignments, library loans, the sources the data came from and the
history of changes to it. Anyone without an account can request the same
export by writing to us.

**Correction.** Write to us, or ask a moderator, to correct anything
that is wrong.

**Erasure.** Anyone may ask to have their personal data removed from
Schoenstatt Link, in part or in whole. A complete erasure removes the
person record, their assignments, loans and account, and removes their
name from every change they made. The one exception is the name of
someone listed as the author, editor, translator or illustrator of a
published work: the name stays on the work, and everything else about
them is removed. An erasure cannot be carried out while a borrowed book
is still out.

Nothing is kept to recognise an erased person later, so their data could
be added again by someone else. A new request removes it again.

**Objection and complaint.** You may object to our keeping your data in
the register, and we will remove it unless the movement's need for it
clearly outweighs yours. If you are not satisfied with our answer, you
may complain to the data-protection authority of the country where you
live.

Where the data is kept
----------------------

The site and its database are hosted by Hetzner Online GmbH, a German
hosting company. The administrator keeps copies for backup and for
developing the site, which may be outside the European Economic Area.
Personal data is never sold, and never given to anyone outside the
administrators.

Personal data is not part of the Creative Commons licence that covers the
rest of the site's content.

Changes to this policy
----------------------

We will record every change to this policy below. For an important
change we may also tell you through other means.

-   2026-09-25: Rewritten. It names who is responsible and the legal
    basis for each kind of data, describes cookies, and describes who can
    see what as the site actually works. It adds the self-service export
    and the rules for erasure, states that contact data is deleted after
    five years (this is now enforced), and removes promises the site
    never kept: per-person telephone and email settings, a hierarchy of
    access by diocese and nation, and an "irreversible" hash kept for
    erased persons.
-   2016-03-15: First version.

